<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">King Leopold's Soliloquy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1905</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">King Leopold's Soliloquy is a satirical pamphlet by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-twain">Mark Twain</a>, published in 1905 in support of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-reform-association">Congo Reform Association</a>. Written as a rambling monologue in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-ii">Leopold II</a> defends his rule of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-free-state">Congo Free State</a>, it quotes the evidence against him and includes photographs of mutilated Congolese, mocking the king's claim of humanitarian purpose.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/king-leopolds-soliloquy.jpg" alt="King Leopold's Soliloquy" width="200" height="294" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mark Twain · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_Leopold%27s_soliloquy.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
